  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 124 AM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern over the desert southwest will begin to evolve \non Tuesday as a deep trough off the coast of California digs \nsouthward and ridging rapidly amplifies over the Central Plains. \nThis will drag a more focused plume of sub-tropical northward from \nthe Gulf of California into Arizona and New Mexico. While there are \nno tropical systems that will get caught within this plume, the \nmoisture itself will be enough to increase the coverage of showers \nand storms mid to late week. Strong ascent and moisture advection \nare expected out ahead of the digging trough, resulting in \nimpressive moisture transport values. Integrated Vapor Transport \n(IVT) will be maximized over western AZ Wednesday, with values \nreaching as high as 600 units (kg/ms). This is very high for the \nIntermountain West, with NAEFS climatology suggesting this would be \nabove the 99th percentile.\n\nThe predominant source of model uncertainty remains the depth of \nthe west coast trough and the speed at which it ejects inland. ENS \nmembers remain on the deeper side, with GEFS members favoring a \nslightly more progressive pattern. Given the strengthening El Nino \nand the MJO being in Phase 1 (western Hemisphere), pattern \nrecognition would suggest that the deeper solutions would be \nfavored, although only time will tell. 50th percentile QPF amounts \nfor the Wednesday-Friday timeframe still show a broad area of 0.5-\n1.5\", with the highest amounts focusing over northwest NM. Amounts \nhave trended down slightly in most areas, but are higher in the \nNorthwest Plateau where it looks like the strongest moisture \ntransport will set up. \n\nThe severe weather threat (or lack thereof) will be highly dependent \non instability. LREF guidance shows the best overlapping of both \ninstability and shear in southern NM Thursday then in southeast NM \non Friday. While disagreement remains high, models are trending \nwetter over the weekend into the early part of next week, with the \npotential for more subtropical moisture to return to New Mexico.",
